WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
<v Narrator>The idea of cultural humility comes from a 1998 paper by Tervalon and Murray-Garcia,

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.350
written for medical education.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.100
They described three commitments.

4
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.650
First, lifelong self-examination and self-critique: continuing to look at how your

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.600
own learned expectations shape what you see.

6
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.550
Second, attention to power: noticing the imbalance between the professional and the

7
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:22.900
person being served.

8
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.250
Third, partnership that benefits both sides and does not decide things for people.

9
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.800
Their setting was doctors and patients.

10
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:36.150
Your setting is different, and the readings behind this course are careful about

11
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.500
that.

12
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.250
They borrow the three ideas, not the clinical procedures.

13
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.600
Self-examination, power and partnership can guide how you answer a call, write a

14
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.950
notice, run a meeting or review a policy.

15
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.100
They do not turn into a legal rule or a step-by-step procedure for every DHS

16
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.450
program.

17
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.400
<v Narrator>One reading uses a story about two friends getting ready for a move.

18
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.750
The first friend learned that accepting practical help shows trust and shared care.

19
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.300
The second friend learned that making arrangements yourself shows responsibility and

20
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.050
avoids imposing on others.

21
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.800
When the second friend declines an offer to help pack, the first friend's first

22
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:26.550
thought is, “My friend does not value our friendship.” The second friend has not

23
00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.900
said that.

24
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:34.050
Neither meaning needs to be tied to a nationality, a family type or a group.

25
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.400
The second friend can value the friendship and still decline this one offer.

26
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:45.550
Knowing their own way of caring cannot tell the first friend what the second friend

27
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.900
intended.

28
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:53.450
A better next step is a question: “Would help feel useful, or would you rather make

29
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.600
the arrangements yourself?” That is the heart of the practice.

30
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.750
You keep your first reading open long enough for the other person's meaning to reach

31
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.100
you.

32
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.850
<v Narrator>Cultural humility is easy to misread, so it helps to say what it is not.

33
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.200
It is not just being modest, or saying you do not know everything.

34
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:23.350
It asks you to look at how your expectations affect an exchange, and whether another

35
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.300
person's view can actually change your response.

36
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.250
It is not giving up your expertise.

37
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.200
A community brief may explain useful history.

38
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.150
Your role may carry knowledge people need.

39
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.300
Neither one gives you complete knowledge of a person's priorities.

40
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.250
It is not agreeing to every request.

41
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.400
You can understand what someone is asking, explain a real limit, and look for an

42
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.750
alternative, all without promising something you cannot provide.

43
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.500
And it does not set aside your real duties.

44
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.450
Humility improves the basis for your response.

45
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.200
It does not replace decisions that belong to the person or area responsible for

46
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.550
them.

47
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.500
<v Narrator>You may have heard cultural humility set against cultural competence, as if one

48
00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.450
knows everything and the other knows nothing.

49
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.000
The readings push back on that.

50
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.550
The 1998 authors challenged the idea of competence as finished mastery.

51
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.700
Hook and his colleagues go further and call mastering facts about groups a goal no

52
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.050
one can reach.

53
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.000
Yet the National Center for Cultural Competence describes its own framework as

54
00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.750
developmental, including self-assessment and adaptation as well as knowledge.

55
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.700
So the two need not be rivals.

56
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.250
One reading shows how they fit.

57
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.400
A person researches an old bowl that a friend inherited.

58
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:53.150
The researcher learns its period, its materials and how such bowls were often used.

59
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.500
That is useful.

60
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:59.850
Then the researcher assumes its owner should keep it on a shelf, untouched.

61
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.600
The owner explains that using it carefully every day keeps a loved person present.

62
00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.950
History and the owner's meaning answer different questions.

63
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.700
The researcher needs both.

64
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:17.050
<v Narrator>Hook and his colleagues put it simply: cultural humility is a stance, not a

65
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.400
credential.

66
00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.550
You do not finish it.

67
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.900
Finishing this course will not mean you understand everyone's culture, and no label

68
00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.850
like humble or competent tells you what someone actually understood or did.

69
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.400
A specific account says far more.

70
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:38.350
The researcher learned the bowl's history and reconsidered an assumption about the

71
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.700
bowl's owner.

72
00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.650
That leaves room for the next question.

73
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.200
The same is true of you.

74
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.350
Each conversation can clarify one interpretation and change one response.

75
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.100
That is real progress.

76
00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:55.650
It is not mastery, and it does not need to be.

77
00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:02.200
Hold on to one line from the readings as you go: knowledge informs the question, it

78
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.750
does not answer for the individual.

79
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.700
Your experience helps you ask better questions.

80
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.850
The person still holds the answer about their own life.

